      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we start by recalling the formula for the magnitude of a complex number. For a complex number \\( a + bi \\), the magnitude is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\). Here, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). Therefore, the magnitude is:\n\n\\[\n|{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). So, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13 = 52\n\\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16 = 36\n\\]\n\nTaking the positive square root of both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
